PURPOSE
-------
Build a Next.js frontend MVP for the Internet Banking Concept microservices platform.
The existing backend provides 7 Spring Boot microservices (user, fund-transfer, utility-payment,
core-banking, api-gateway, config-server, service-registry) with no frontend. This task
creates a complete working demo web application so the platform can be showcased end-to-end.


QUESTIONS & ANSWERS
-------------------
Q1: Which frontend technology should we use?
A1: Next.js

Q2: Which flows should the MVP cover?
A2: All five:
    - Login / authentication (via Keycloak)
    - Dashboard (account balance, recent transactions)
    - Fund transfer between accounts
    - Utility bill payment
    - User profile


DECISIONS MADE
--------------
1. Use Next.js (App Router) with TypeScript — matches modern Next.js conventions and gives us
   server components, layouts, and built-in routing.
2. Use NextAuth.js with Keycloak provider for OAuth2/OIDC authentication — the backend already
   uses Keycloak 23; NextAuth handles token lifecycle and session management cleanly.
3. Use Tailwind CSS for styling — zero-config, utility-first, fast to build a professional UI.
4. Use shadcn/ui component library (built on Radix UI + Tailwind) — gives accessible, polished
   components without heavy bundle cost.
5. All API calls route through the existing API Gateway at port 8082 — no direct calls to
   downstream services from the frontend.
6. Place the frontend at `internet-banking-frontend/` in the repo root, parallel to the existing
   Spring Boot service directories.
7. Use React Query (TanStack Query) for server state / data fetching — cleaner than raw fetch
   with loading/error states built in.
8. Environment variables in `.env.local` for gateway URL, Keycloak realm/client config.


ALTERNATIVES CONSIDERED
-----------------------
- Angular: Heavier setup, less ecosystem momentum for rapid MVP; rejected in favour of Next.js.
- Create React App: Deprecated; no SSR capability; rejected.
- Axios instead of React Query: React Query provides caching + loading states with less boilerplate; kept fetch/React Query.
- Calling downstream services directly: Bypasses gateway security; rejected — all calls go via port 8082.


SCOPE OF CHANGES
----------------
Affected services: internet-banking-api-gateway (CORS config may need updating), new internet-banking-frontend module
Affected layers  : New Next.js app — pages, components, hooks, API client layer
New endpoints    : None on backend — frontend consumes existing endpoints
Schema changes   : None
New dependencies : Next.js 14, NextAuth.js, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, TanStack Query, lucide-react


RISKS & NOTES
-------------
- CORS: The API gateway must allow requests from the Next.js dev origin (localhost:3000).
  May need to add CORS configuration to the gateway.
- Keycloak client: A public OIDC client for the frontend needs to exist in Keycloak realm
  (or we reuse the existing one). The docker-compose setup includes a pre-loaded Keycloak realm.
- Token forwarding: NextAuth stores the access token in the session; all API calls must
  attach it as `Authorization: Bearer {token}` to pass through the gateway.
- The docker-compose environment must be running for the frontend to function.


ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
-------------------
- [ ] Login page authenticates via Keycloak and redirects to dashboard on success
- [ ] Dashboard shows account balance and last 10 transactions
- [ ] Fund transfer form submits and shows success/error feedback
- [ ] Utility payment form submits and shows success/error feedback
- [ ] User profile page displays current user details
- [ ] Logout clears session and returns to login
- [ ] All pages are protected (unauthenticated users redirected to login)
- [ ] Next.js app builds without errors (`npm run build`)
- [ ] API gateway CORS updated to allow frontend origin


